00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.210
that do that.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:56.470
And once you do that,
you have a known endpoint

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:57.580
and a group of computers.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.310
And we call that
group in Compute

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:02.850
Engine a managed instance group.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:04.600
Then you can put a
bunch of logic on that.

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.980
So it's actually a
service in and of itself.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:10.242
So it handles starting up new
machines when they're needed.

00:11:10.242 --> 00:11:12.700
If you turn the dial up and
you're like Oh, I have five now

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:15.730
and I want to have 10,
it starts the new ones.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:17.680
That can be set up to
be run automatically

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.330
depending on the load you get.

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:22.658
And then you can spread
those out across the world.

00:11:22.658 --> 00:11:24.700
You can have some of them
running in one country,

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:26.200
some of them running
somewhere else,

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.350
and route the traffic to the
closest one for your users,

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.890
that sort of thing.

